// The number scanner, exercised through `parse` -- `P` and `scan_number` are
// private to `std/json`, so a fixture has no other route to them.
//
// Each accepted case prints a label and what `stringify` produces from the
// parsed value, so the golden pins the whole path at once: the span the scanner
// matched, the `Float` the intrinsic produced from that span, and the
// rendering. The expected renderings were derived from JSON's grammar and
// Rust's `f64` Display, not captured from this program's output -- `1e3` is one
// thousand and renders `1000`, `1.5E-2` is fifteen thousandths and renders
// `0.015`, and `-0` keeps its sign because an f64 negative zero is a distinct
// bit pattern that Display shows.
//
// `report` takes a LABEL as well as the source text because two inputs below
// cannot be echoed: leading and trailing spaces are invisible in a golden, and
// a form feed would put a raw control byte into the `.stdout` file. For every
// other case the label is the source text, so those lines read as before.
//
// Each rejected case prints the message and the character index. `01`, `+1` and
// `.5` each produce TWO failures -- the scanner's, then `parse`'s
// trailing-content check on the remainder the scanner did not consume -- so
// those three goldens are what pins `fail`'s first-failure-wins guard: without
// it the printed message would be the trailing-content one. `1e` and `1.`
// consume to the end of the input and so produce exactly one failure each.
//
// The last two cases are the whole of `skip_ws`'s coverage, in both directions.
// `' 1 '` is the only input whose leading whitespace must be SKIPPED, so it is
// the only one that executes the `bump()` inside `skip_ws`; narrowing that
// predicate turns it into `expected a digit at 0`. The form feed is the other
// direction: RFC 8259 admits exactly space, tab, line feed and carriage return
// as whitespace, so a form feed must NOT be skipped, and widening the predicate
// to admit it turns this line from an error into `1`.
fn main() {
    report("0", "0")
    report("-0", "-0")
    report("12", "12")
    report("-3.5", "-3.5")
    report("1e3", "1e3")
    report("1.5E-2", "1.5E-2")
    report("2.5", "2.5")

    report("01", "01")
    report("+1", "+1")
    report(".5", ".5")
    report("1e", "1e")
    report("1.", "1.")

    report("' 1 '", " 1 ")
    report("'\\f1'", "\u{0C}1")
}

fn report(label: String, src: String) {
    match parse(src) {
        Ok(v) => println("${label} -> ${stringify(v)}")
        Err(e) => println("${label} -> error: ${e.msg} at ${e.at}")
    }
}
